The program itself is small: read one [38;5;10mint[0m with [38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m, then dump its 4 in-memory bytes as lowercase hex plus a newline. Nearly all the fidelity work was in [38;5;10mscanf[0m, which has non-obvious behavior worth calling out:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mglibc's [38;5;10m%d[0m is [38;5;10mstrtol[0m followed by a truncating store into an [38;5;10mint[0m.[22m That means out-of-range input does not saturate at [38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m — it saturates at the [3m64-bit[23m boundary and then gets its low 32 bits taken. I confirmed this against the compiled C before writing any Rust:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
| input | output | why |[0m[0m
|---|---|---|[0m[0m
| [38;5;10m2147483648[0m | [38;5;10m00000080[0m | fits in [38;5;10mlong[0m, truncates to [38;5;10mINT_MIN[0m |[0m[0m
| [38;5;10m4294967296[0m | [38;5;10m00000000[0m | low 32 bits are zero |[0m[0m
| [38;5;10m9223372036854775808[0m | [38;5;10mffffffff[0m | clamps to [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m, truncates to [38;5;10m-1[0m |[0m[0m
| [38;5;10m-9223372036854775809[0m | [38;5;10m00000000[0m | clamps to [38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m, truncates to [38;5;10m0[0m |[0m[0m
